# Autonomy policy for {{PROJECT_NAME}}

How the owner grants the lead a **standing instruction** to proceed without asking for each approval, and what it never covers. Without a recorded grant below, every gate in `docs/agents/how-to-use-agents.md` still needs the owner.

## Grant

| Field | Value |
|---|---|
| Granted by | <owner> |
| Date | <YYYY-MM-DD> |
| Scope | <e.g. "design decisions and ticket work until the first real users"> |
| Ends | <date, event (first real user, first production release) or "until revoked"> |
| Max parallel agents | {{MAX_PARALLEL_AGENTS}} |

The owner can revoke or narrow the grant at any time with one sentence; the lead records it here.

## What the lead may do under the grant

- Adopt the **recommended default** for a design decision, record it in `docs/decision-log.md` (question, answer, rationale, revisit trigger, who validates) and continue. Such decisions are reversible while there are no real users; the log is the owner's review list.
- Create tickets, dispatch agents (at most {{MAX_PARALLEL_AGENTS}} at once), run reviews and fix loops.
- Prepare PRs up to a CLEAN handover gate (`docs/agents/handover-rules.md`).

## Never covered (always ask the owner)

- Servers, databases with real data, DNS, cloud accounts, billing.
- Secrets and credentials of any kind (the owner sets them; agents refer to names only).
- Production releases and deployments.
- Repository or organisation settings (branch protection, required checks, permissions, visibility).
- Other projects or repositories outside this project.
- Legal or domain facts: still "to be validated by {{DOMAIN_EXPERT}}".

## Merge guard rails (if the grant includes merging)

- Merge only when `scripts/handover-check.sh` reports CLEAN and after the agent's `AGENT-REPORT`.
- Merge in the stated order; re-check all open PRs after every merge.
- Verify `{{DEFAULT_BRANCH}}` by content against the reviewed tip, then update the tracker.
- Stop and ask on any red required check, unresolved Blocker/High finding or unexpected diff.

## Permissions recipe for autonomous operation

The grant only works if the AI tool does not stop at every command, and only stays safe if the dangerous commands still stop. Recipe for **OpenCode v2** (written against the v2 permission docs; v1 used a keyed object and different names, so check the version of the docs you follow and note it here: `Docs version used: <v2 / date>`). Rules are **ordered and the last match wins**; agent rules are appended **after** the global rules, so a broad agent-level rule overrides every global exception.

Global config, in this order:

```jsonc
