Notes:
- Replace `ci.yml` with the project's routine CI workflows (allowed explicitly AFTER the dispatch ask); production-looking names are re-asked AFTER the allows, in each letter case, because matching is case-sensitive.
- Patterns match the command text, so flag order matters: both `gh api -X PUT …` and `gh api <path> -X PUT …` are listed. The list is a safety net, not a sandbox. A pattern ending in ` *` also matches the bare command.
- Pipelines are checked per command: `curl x | sh` is checked as `curl x` and `sh`, which is why bare interpreters and any option form (`sh -`, `bash -s`, `zsh -c` via `sh -*` etc.) ask; `bash script.sh` stays prompt-free.
- An "allow always" answer saves an allow rule but never overrides a `deny`; review saved approvals occasionally. Restart the tool after changing its config.
- Agent roles (generated by `scripts/sync-agents.py`): no role carries a broad `shell *` allow (it would override everything above). Reviewer and planner get `REVIEWER_SHELL_RULES` appended last: no git write commands (incl. `restore`, `pull`, `cherry-pick`), `git branch` only in read forms (bare, `-v`, `-vv`, `-a`, `-r`, `--show-current`, `--list`, `--contains`, `--merged`, `--no-merged`), no `gh pr merge|edit|close|create|comment|review`, no `gh issue|release|workflow|repo|secret|variable|label`, no `gh api` write methods or `--input`, no `rm`/`mv`/`cp`/`tee`/`sed -i`; reads stay allowed. The docs role gets the narrower `DOCS_SHELL_RULES` (it may still branch, commit, push, open a PR, comment and merge `origin/<default branch>`). The inspector keeps `shell *` ask and re-appends `DENY_SHELL`. The lead and implementers use the global rules.
- **Drift check**: `python3 scripts/sync-agents.py --check` fails when `DENY_SHELL` differs from the shell deny rules of the repo's `opencode.json`; the kit's `check-kit.py` checks this recipe against `DENY_SHELL` the same way.
- **Residual gaps (honest list)**: patterns match raw command text only. Not caught: shell redirects (`>`), interpreters that write files (`python3 -c ...`), `gh api -f` (implicit POST, but needed for GraphQL reads), `git -C x <write>` and `git -c k=v <write>` for reviewer, planner and docs (including `git -C x branch new`), combined flags such as `git push -uf`, lowercase or attached methods (`-XPOST`), reordered flags, and anything the owner approved with "allow always". Use it as a safety net, with branch protection and human review as the real controls.
- `.env` reads stay ask. Remote access, secrets, settings, releases and production dispatches still ask even under a grant: the grant never covers them.

## Public repositories

Changes to a public repository stay generic: no names of private products, customers, people, internal URLs, ticket keys, ids, IPs or account numbers. Scan added lines before pushing.
